WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.448 --> 00:00:20.455
Hello welcome back to Superstars in History, the talk show that features famous people from the past.

2
00:00:20.955 --> 00:00:22.656
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

3
00:00:23.396 --> 00:00:29.318
Today we're talking with a courageous woman who took a bus ride that helped change America.

4
00:00:30.319 --> 00:00:37.267
She didn't take discrimination sitting down, and her bravery inspired many others in the fight for equality.

5
00:00:38.147 --> 00:00:43.370
Please help me welcome Rosa Parks!

6
00:00:56.738 --> 00:00:57.679
Thank you, Faye.

7
00:00:58.079 --> 00:00:59.080
It's nice to be here.

8
00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:06.235
You're most famous for a very fateful bus ride, and I want to hear all about that day.

9
00:01:06.735 --> 00:01:10.597
But before that, why don't you tell us about the world you grew up in?

10
00:01:11.617 --> 00:01:18.120
I was born in 1913 and grew up in and around Montgomery, Alabama, during the "Jim Crow" days.

11
00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:24.683
Jim Crow was a system of segregation laws and an unwritten code about how black people were to

12
00:01:24.723 --> 00:01:25.904
be treated in the South.

13
00:01:26.656 --> 00:01:32.118
Blacks went to different schools, ate in different restaurants, and drank from separate water fountains.

14
00:01:32.918 --> 00:01:36.279
We also had to sit in different parts of buses and trains.

15
00:01:37.499 --> 00:01:40.740
Segregation laws were only part of the problem, right?

16
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.380
That's right.

17
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.922
There were also terrorist groups like the Ku Klux Klan that targeted black people.

18
00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:55.624
By the time I was six, the Klan was riding through the black community, burning churches, beating people,

19
00:01:56.104 --> 00:01:56.845
and murdering.

20
00:01:57.905 --> 00:02:00.747
My grandfather told us to go to bed with our clothes on.

21
00:02:01.227 --> 00:02:06.050
That way if the Klan broke into our house, we would be ready to run and get out

22
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.692
fast. That must have been really scary.

23
00:02:10.472 --> 00:02:14.294
And back then it wasn't easy for black people to vote for better conditions.

24
00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:18.656
The white people in charge made it difficult for blacks to vote.

25
00:02:19.297 --> 00:02:25.732
They made us take ridiculous tests—guess the number of jelly beans in a jar, things like that—before we

26
00:02:25.772 --> 00:02:26.253
could vote.

27
00:02:27.233 --> 00:02:31.895
They also charged a fee for voting, and most blacks were too poor to pay.

28
00:02:33.095 --> 00:02:38.258
Not being able to vote meant we couldn't elect leaders who would work to change the system.

29
00:02:38.938 --> 00:02:40.358
We had to change it ourselves.

30
00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:44.120
Did you start by refusing to move to the back of the bus?

31
00:02:45.521 --> 00:02:50.843
Well, that's what I was best known for—and it was a very important incident.

32
00:02:51.803 --> 00:02:53.204
But it wasn't how I started.

33
00:02:53.928 --> 00:02:59.413
By the 1950s I had been working for years with civil rights groups that were trying to make

34
00:02:59.493 --> 00:03:00.634
sure blacks could vote.

35
00:03:01.794 --> 00:03:07.039
I also helped raise money for the legal defense of people who were arrested for resisting Jim Crow

36
00:03:07.079 --> 00:03:10.101
laws—of course, I became one of those people!

37
00:03:11.142 --> 00:03:13.163
Tell us more about that day on the bus.

38
00:03:14.685 --> 00:03:18.348
It was December 1, 1955.

39
00:03:19.208 --> 00:03:23.632
At that point blacks had not demanded that buses be desegregated.

40
00:03:24.552 --> 00:03:26.994
We had just asked to be treated with more respect.

41
00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:31.617
I was on my way home from work as a seamstress for a department store.

42
00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:35.720
I took a seat just behind the "whites only" section of the bus.

43
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.423
But when the bus got too full of white people, the bus driver told me to move further

44
00:03:40.483 --> 00:03:42.825
back so a white man could have my seat.

45
00:03:43.905 --> 00:03:44.806
I just sat there.

46
00:03:45.607 --> 00:03:47.988
He shouted at me and told me to move.

47
00:03:48.949 --> 00:03:49.910
I said, "No."

48
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.232
Then he told me he would have me arrested.

49
00:03:54.104 --> 00:03:56.106
I said, "You can do that."

50
00:03:56.906 --> 00:03:58.828
That was so brave!

51
00:04:00.009 --> 00:04:06.513
Well, other black people had refused to give up their seats for whites before—I had done it before.

52
00:04:07.314 --> 00:04:09.976
But they usually just threw us off the bus.

53
00:04:11.077 --> 00:04:15.760
When this driver said he would have me arrested, I knew that if I let them take me

54
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.222
to jail, we might be able to build a movement around this.

55
00:04:20.423 --> 00:04:20.984
And we did!

56
00:04:22.432 --> 00:04:24.813
The local black community, led by Dr.

57
00:04:24.893 --> 00:04:26.713
Martin Luther King Jr.

58
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.714
organized a bus boycott.

59
00:04:29.194 --> 00:04:33.535
To protest my arrest, black people refused to ride on city buses.

60
00:04:34.456 --> 00:04:39.677
The leaders who organized the boycott intended it to only last one day, but the people of the

61
00:04:39.697 --> 00:04:43.158
community kept it up even after I was released from jail.

62
00:04:44.118 --> 00:04:46.979
That's amazing how the community came together.

63
00:04:46.999 --> 00:04:49.500
It wasn't easy.

64
00:04:50.124 --> 00:04:53.806
People had to get to work somehow, and few blacks at the time had cars.

65
00:04:54.586 --> 00:04:59.588
Black churches raised money to buy station wagons and formed car pools to take people to work.

66
00:05:00.648 --> 00:05:04.190
The white leaders of the city of Montgomery were not happy.

67
00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:09.032
The city buses were losing a lot of money because we weren't riding them.

68
00:05:09.852 --> 00:05:16.075
The police harassed the car pool drivers, giving them tickets for traffic violations even when they hadn't committed

69
00:05:16.295 --> 00:05:19.076
one. I got many death threats.

70
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:20.981
Dr. King's home was bombed.

71
00:05:21.741 --> 00:05:24.003
Fortunately he and his family weren't harmed.

72
00:05:24.523 --> 00:05:27.384
But we carried on brave and strong.

73
00:05:27.945 --> 00:05:32.947
The boycott lasted for 381 days.

74
00:05:35.449 --> 00:05:36.990
That's more than a year!

75
00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:38.410
How did it end?

76
00:05:39.331 --> 00:05:45.874
Well, while we were all protesting and refusing to ride the buses, the movement's lawyers were working in

77
00:05:45.894 --> 00:05:48.596
the courts to challenge the segregation laws.

78
00:05:49.436 --> 00:05:58.662
Then on December 20, 1956, a Supreme Court ruling went into effect that made bus segregation illegal.

79
00:05:59.083 --> 00:05:59.843
We had won.

80
00:06:02.525 --> 00:06:04.086
Was that the end of the movement?

81
00:06:05.507 --> 00:06:06.387
Oh no!

82
00:06:06.547 --> 00:06:07.728
It was the beginning.

83
00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:15.493
The bus boycott showed black Americans that if we could stand up to unfairness and inequality, things would

84
00:06:15.553 --> 00:06:19.176
change. The civil rights movement continued to grow.

85
00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.501
We got many new laws passed to protect our rights.

86
00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:30.383
For example, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 made it illegal to discriminate against blacks.

87
00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:35.445
The Voting Rights Act of 1965 protected our right to vote.

88
00:06:36.585 --> 00:06:40.586
As for me and my husband, after the bus boycott we both lost our jobs.

89
00:06:41.407 --> 00:06:45.168
Eventually we moved north to Detroit, Michigan, to find work.

90
00:06:46.416 --> 00:06:49.979
So did you give up activism and settle down to a peaceful life?

91
00:06:51.301 --> 00:06:52.061
Not quite.

92
00:06:52.562 --> 00:06:54.824
I guess I was just born to be a troublemaker.

93
00:06:56.565 --> 00:07:00.689
I continued to work in the civil rights movement and stayed involved in politics.

94
00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:04.212
For many years I was an assistant to a U.S.

95
00:07:04.272 --> 00:07:05.033
Congress member.

96
00:07:05.733 --> 00:07:09.377
I kept working for good causes as long as I could!

97
00:07:10.538 --> 00:07:12.980
You are truly an inspiration.

98
00:07:13.784 --> 00:07:16.205
Thank you so much for joining us today, Mrs.

99
00:07:16.245 --> 00:07:19.546
Parks. We'll let you get back to history now.

100
00:07:20.766 --> 00:07:21.766
Thank you, Faye.

101
00:07:26.448 --> 00:07:33.050
Rosa Parks spent most of her life fighting injustice and inspired a generation of activists to do the

102
00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:40.112
same. In 1996 President Bill Clinton awarded her the Presidential Medal of Freedom.

103
00:07:41.168 --> 00:07:44.269
She died in 2005 at the age of 92.

104
00:07:45.269 --> 00:07:47.830
Her body lay in state at the U.S.

105
00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:54.012
Capitol. She was the first woman and the second African American to be honored in this way.

106
00:07:55.032 --> 00:07:56.892
I hope you enjoyed today's interview.

107
00:07:58.133 --> 00:08:01.494
I'll be back soon with another famous face from history!
